as. It's about a 2-km uphill walk from the city center, so you may prefer to take local bus 6 instead, which runs every 20 minutes.","wikidata":null},{"type":"see","name":"Tomb of Rachel","alt":null,"lat":32.780478,"lng":35.541919,"price":null,"priceClass":null,"hours":null,"url":null,"phone":null,"address":null,"content":"Not the Biblical Rachel. This Rachel is the wife of Rabbi Akiva, who is buried in a different part of town.","wikidata":"Q12410950"},{"type":"see","name":"Tomb of the Rambam","alt":"Maimonides","lat":32.79,"lng":35.5372,"price":null,"priceClass":null,"hours":null,"url":"http://mhcny.org/?page=museum","phone":null,"address":null,"content":"This revered rabbi, who died in 1204, was one of 12th-century Egypt's most highly regarded sages, while working as a doctor in the court of the Muslim ruler Saladin. Legend has it that before his death in Cairo, he instructed followers to load his remains onto a camel and bury him wherever the camel expired. The camel was apparently drawn to Tiberias. Next to the grave is the Maimonides Heritage Center museum. Also b","wikidata":"Q625219"},{"type":"see","name":"Tomb of Meir Ba'al Hanes","alt":null,"lat":32.766211,"lng":35.549819,"price":null,"priceClass":null,"hours":null,"url":null,"phone":null,"address":null,"content":"This 2nd-century rabbi, who helped to compile the Mishnah, is buried here. The tomb is marked by two synagogues: Sephardic (on the left with the white dome) and Ashkenazic (with the blue dome). In the courtyard of the Sephardic synagogue is a pillar topped by a large bowl, and four days before the Lag B'Omer holiday a bonfire is lit here on the Pesah Sheni (second Passover) holiday. Crowds of religious Jews visit thr","wikidata":"Q6653557"},{"type":"see","name":"The Antiquities Museum","alt":null,"lat":null,"lng":null,"price":null,"priceClass":null,"hours":null,"url":null,"phone":null,"address":null,"content":"Housed in the Fishermen's Mosque, now under renovation.","wikidata":null},{"type":"see","name":"Dona Gracia Museum","alt":null,"lat":32.7866817,"lng":35.5383474,"price":null,"priceClass":null,"hours":null,"url":null,"phone":"+972 4 672 8900","address":"HaPrachim 3","content":"Dona Gracia Nasi was a wealthy 16th-century Jewish woman. In 1558, the Ottoman Empire granted her control of the Tiberias region in exchange for an increase in tax revenue. She began to rebuild the abandoned villages in the area, hoping that Jewish refugees would be able to settle there. However, she died not too long afterwards in 1569. She is seen as an early predecessor of the Zionist movement, and all the more no","wikidata":null},{"type":"see","name":"The Khan","alt":null,"lat":32.787705,"lng":35.540852,"price":null,"priceClass":null,"hours":null,"url":null,"phone":null,"address":null,"content":"This used to be Tiberias' central square, with a mosque at its centre.","wikidata":null},{"type":"see","name":"Tiberias Castle/Fortress","alt":"The Citadel","lat":32.790172,"lng":35.540718,"price":null,"priceClass":null,"hours":null,"url":null,"phone":null,"address":null,"content":"The Turkish citadel was the highest point in the old town. It now houses an art gallery.","wikidata":"Q12407331"},{"type":"see","name":"St Peter's Church","alt":null,"lat":32.788226,"lng":35.54253,"price":null,"priceClass":null,"hours":null,"url":null,"phone":null,"address":null,"content":"Hidden along the northern promenade, it is worth looking out for the lovely Franciscan church built by 12th-century Crusaders. The Muslims converted it into a mosque, and you can make out an area of uneven stone on the southern wall filling in the hole where a mihrab (prayer niche indicating the direction of Mecca) was carved. Later, the Turks used the building as a caravanserai before it was rebuilt as a church in 1","wikidata":"Q6872202"},{"type":"see","name":"The Scottish Compound","alt":null,"lat":32.788988,"lng":35.541501,"price":null,"priceClass":null,"hours":null,"url":null,"phone":null,"address":null,"content":"This was a small Scottish colony during the 19th century. It now houses a boutique hotel and a church.","wikidata":"Q7435781"},{"type":"see","name":"Al-Amari Mosque","alt":null,"lat":32.787845,"lng":35.541159,"price":null,"priceClass":null,"hours":null,"url":null,"phone":null,"address":null,"content":"As out of place as a pin-stripe suited gent at a teenage rave, the dignified little mosque looks threatened and lost squeezed between some gaudy shops and a brusque concrete supermarket. Built by Daher al-Omar in the mid-18th century, the mosque is one of the very few buildings in Tiberias that predates 1948. It is generally held that its construction was partly paid for by the town's Jewish community, presumably gra","wikidata":"Q6707341"},{"type":"see","name":"Old Cemetery","alt":null,"lat":32.780961,"lng":35.543,"price":null,"priceClass":null,"hours":null,"url":null,"phone":null,"address":null,"content":"With Jewish and Muslim sections.","wikidata":"Q7047088"},{"type":"see","name":"Roman theater","alt":"Khirbet Qasra Atabraa","lat":32.773611,"lng":35.544167,"price":null,"priceClass":null,"hours":null,"url":null,"phone":null,"address":null,"content":"Contains ruins from the Roman (1st century) and Arab (11th century) periods, as well as a playground for kids.","wikidata":"Q3966846"},{"type":"see","name":"Berko Archaeological Park","alt":null,"lat":32.772826,"lng":35.546215,"price":null,"priceClass":null,"hours":null,"url":null,"phone":null,"address":null,"content":"Contains ruins from the Roman (1st century) and Arab (11th century) periods, as well as a playground for kids.","wikidata":null},{"type":"see","name":"Hamath Tiberias National Park","alt":null,"lat":32.765733,"lng":35.551665,"price":"₪14/12/7 Adult/Student/Child","priceClass":null,"hours":"8:00-16:00 (closes earlier in holidays)","url":"http://www.parks.org.il","phone":null,"address":null,"content":"Israel's spa craze actually has a 2000-year-old history which started at the hot springs of Hammat Tiberias when, during Roman times, they were the focus, if not raison d'être, of a community of 40,000 fervent bathers. Check out the history of the site at the Hammat Tiberias National Park, which features a small museum in what was part of a Turkish bathhouse. The main highlight is a synagogue dating from the 3rd to 5","wikidata":"Q7067506"},{"type":"see","name":"Sea of Galilee fountain-statue","alt":null,"lat":32.78564,"lng":35.54417,"price":null,"priceClass":null,"hours":null,"url":null,"phone":null,"address":null,"content":"Showing water level and shape of the Sea of Galilee.","wikidata":null},{"type":"see","name":"The Jewish Court","alt":null,"lat":null,"lng":null,"price":null,"priceClass":null,"hours":null,"url":null,"phone":null,"address":null,"content":"The site of three 19th-century synagogues at the heart of the old town.","wikidata":null},{"type":"do","name":"Aqua Kef","alt":null,"lat":32.775,"lng":35.54649,"price":null,"priceClass":null,"hours":null,"url":"https://www.aquakef.com/en/main/","phone":null,"address":null,"content":"A water park which floats on the Sea of Galilee. Check that it is open before coming (probably closed in winter).","wikidata":null},{"type":"do","name":"Private Hot Springs Tour","alt":null,"lat":null,"lng":null,"price":"₪50 pP","priceClass":null,"hours":null,"url":null,"phone":null,"address":null,"content":"Tiberias Hostel offers private spa tours to the hot springs south of the lake. The trip goes to some unofficial springs, not the public ones with entry fee. This tour includes beer and goes when there are at least three people.","wikidata":null},{"type":"buy","name":"Fish market","alt":null,"lat":32.786906,"lng":35.540774,"price":null,"priceClass":null,"hours":null,"url":null,"phone":null,"address":null,"content":null,"wikidata":null},{"type":"buy","name":"The promenade","alt":null,"lat":32.788286,"lng":35.542896,"price":null,"priceClass":null,"hours":null,"url":null,"phone":null,"address":null,"content":null,"wikidata":null},{"type":"